VERDICT
Correct: your code extracts each digit with modulo 10 and tests divisibility against the original number with optimal logarithmic time and constant auxiliary memory.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well:

You preserved the original number by making a copy in n so that num % digit always tests against the initial value.

You used the standard, optimal digit extraction loop condition (n != 0).

You updated the answer count cleanly inside the condition.

Is it correct?

Yes, it is fully correct and passes all test cases on LeetCode.

Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses:

Under LeetCode's constraints (1 <= num <= 10^9 and num does not contain 0), there are no bugs.

Important general edge case: If the input could contain the digit 0 (for example, num = 102), num % digit would divide by zero and trigger a runtime crash (Floating Point Exception). In real-world code or interviews without that constraint, always guard with digit != 0.

Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason:

Time complexity: O(log10(num)) because dividing n by 10 cuts off one decimal digit every loop iteration.

Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space because only three simple integer variables (n, count, digit) are stored.

Why my approach works:

Take num = 124 as an example. First iteration: digit is 4; 124 % 4 == 0 is true, count becomes 1, n becomes 12. Second iteration: digit is 2; 124 % 2 == 0 is true, count becomes 2, n becomes 1. Third iteration: digit is 1; 124 % 1 == 0 is true, count becomes 3, n becomes 0. Loop stops and returns 3.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Convert to String (Brute Force)

Where it comes from:

Common beginner approach found in LeetCode discussion forums and introductory programming lessons.

Idea:

Convert the integer to a string using std::to_string. Walk through every character, convert it back to a digit by subtracting the character '0', and check if it divides the number.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>

class Solution {
public:
    int countDigits(int num) {
        std::string s = std::to_string(num);
        int count = 0;
        for (char ch : s) {
            int digit = ch - '0';
            if (num % digit == 0) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(log10(num)) to build the string and scan each character.

Space Complexity:

O(log10(num)) auxiliary heap space to hold the string characters.

What makes the next approach better:

Converting an integer to a string allocates unnecessary buffer memory on the heap. We can extract digits directly using elementary math.

Approach 2: Direct Modulo and Division (Your Approach — Optimal)

Where it comes from:

Official LeetCode editorial and classic textbook digit extraction.

Idea:

Keep a copy of the number. Extract the last digit with % 10, check divisibility against the original number, and drop the last digit with / 10 until nothing remains.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    int countDigits(int num) {
        int n = num;
        int count = 0;
        while (n > 0) {
            int digit = n % 10;
            if (num % digit == 0) {
                count++;
            }
            n /= 10;
        }
        return count;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(log10(num)) since it performs one iteration per digit (at most 9 to 10 iterations for a 32-bit int).

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space using purely scalar variables.

What makes the next approach better:

For numbers with many repeated digits (like 22224), Approach 2 runs the modulo operation num % 2 four separate times. Modulo/division instructions are relatively slow on CPUs. We can cache digit frequencies to run at most 9 modulo checks.

Approach 3: Digit Frequency Pre-Counting (Micro-Optimized Modulo Calls)

Where it comes from:

Advanced community optimizations reducing hardware division cycles on LeetCode.

Idea:

Digits can only range from 1 to 9. Count how many times each digit appears first into a tiny fixed array of size 10. Then check divisibility for each unique digit 1 through 9 once. If it divides num, add its frequency count to the answer.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    int countDigits(int num) {
        int freq[10] = {0};
        int n = num;
        while (n > 0) {
            freq[n % 10]++;
            n /= 10;
        }
        int count = 0;
        for (int d = 1; d <= 9; d++) {
            if (freq[d] > 0 && num % d == 0) {
                count += freq[d];
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(log10(num)) to count frequencies, but caps the expensive num % d operations to at most 9 checks.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space using a fixed 10-element integer array.

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use?

You used Approach 2 (Direct Modulo and Division).

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem?

Interviewers expect Approach 2. It shows you know how to extract digits using pure math without depending on std::to_string.

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:

Question 1: What happens if num contains the digit 0 (e.g. num = 102)?

Answer: Dividing or doing modulo by 0 causes undefined behavior / Floating Point Exception crash. You must add a safety check: if (digit != 0 && num % digit == 0).

Question 2: What if num can be negative?

Answer: In C++, modulo on a negative integer retains the sign (e.g., -12 % 10 is -2). Take std::abs(num) first before processing.

Question 3: What if the number is huge and given as a string of 100,000 digits?

Answer: We cannot parse it into a standard 64-bit integer. Instead, count the frequency of each digit 1 through 9 across the string in O(L) time, then verify divisibility for each present digit using standard divisibility rules (e.g., check last digit for 2 and 5, sum of digits for 3 and 9, last two digits for 4, rolling remainder for 7).

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

The key insight, in one sentence:

Peeling digits from right to left using % 10 and / 10 allows you to test each digit individually while keeping the original number intact.

My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time:

Always consider division-by-zero whenever a digit can potentially be 0.

Always preserve the original number in a copy variable if you need to perform operations against the whole number later.

Prefer compound assignment operators like n /= 10 and count++ for cleaner code.

The pattern or idea this question is really about:

Digit Extraction and Modulo Arithmetic.

